A||||6||BigTurbo87GT||Rebuilt front end, toe'd out ??||||1||
Z||000000||BigTurbo87GT||10-31-2000||01:16 PM||||Among the many projects I've done so far with my GT, this one had the most unexpected result. I replaced the balljoints and all bushings in the front end. Replaced springs with higher rate, lowering springs and new shocks, new calipers,pads,rotors (painted everything pretty red and yellow too). Looked fantastic, dropped the car off the stands and looked really sweet, no more huge gap above tires. Now here's the part I don't entirely understand, both tires are toe'd out about 8-12*. How did this happen? The changes I expected were maybe camber and/or caster because of the new bushings, adjustable upper balljoint and lowering.<BR>Anybody else have this experience? I don't believe anything is wrong, I'm just curious how I changed this particular dimension of the suspension geometry with the things I did.    Thanks for any insight, Bill.||||reg||1
Z||000001||robertjay||10-31-2000||02:16 PM||||I'm not positive, but if you look at your tie rods, as the suspension moves up and down, the closer they get to straight out of the rack, they will push your wheels out on the front, creating the toe-out.  You lowered your car, so the tie rods are closer to straight out than what they were before, so you should get some toe-out.<P>The toe-in and toe-out that occurs while you are going over bumps and into corners is called bump steer, very common.  Later!<p>[This message has been edited by robertjay (edited 10-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||10-31-2000||02:32 PM||||You'll want to have the alignment checked if you haven't already.  A toe'd out front end will make your car want to wander all over the place and wear tires over time.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||10-31-2000||02:37 PM||||As stated above that is exactly what happened. The geometry of the suspension changed and the tie rods pulled the toe out of whack. When I lowered my car and changed everything on the suspension I had the same problem. The easiest way to get the alignment close is to measure the front and rear of the tires together. measure at about the 9 o'clock and 3 o'clock positions using the outside edge of the left and right tire. This will allow you to get the toe close enough to drive. As for camber a simple level will get you close. It took a suspesion shop almost 4 hours to get my alignment set after I changed evrything.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||BigTurbo87GT||11-01-2000||04:09 PM||||The tie rods make perfect sense as the cause, I should have been able to figure that out.  Thanks guys!||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FST2M6||11-01-2000||04:27 PM||||off topic...  what size is your big turbo? T6? T7?or a Mitsu unit? 20g? <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fstsig1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-01-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||BigTurbo87GT||11-02-2000||04:59 PM||||It's a Holset H1C. I'm still trying to find flow numbers for it. It's physically much larger than any other turbo I've had before.<BR>(Stealth TT, Eclipse GST, Impulse T).<BR>I don't know just yet that it will actually make sense on a 2.8. There may be entirely too much lag (housing volume). At the very least I will have to be very carefull to maintain velocity/temp of exhaust. Turbo was designed for a 5.9L Diesel. Doing rough calc/guestimates, I won't see much boost till 3K rpm or so but from 4K to 6K rpm the turbo should be able to make it's full boost (22lbs).   Still a lot to figure out.||||reg||1
